//? Title: Borwein Pi: Quartic Convergence and the Double-Precision Plateau
//? Subtitle: Actual update records, fourth-root cancellation and an exact-arithmetic error model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Borwein's quartic iteration approaches pi rapidly in exact arithmetic, but a
double stores only a finite number of significant bits. We retain the original
four-update sequence and inspect the native loop's actual states. Two updates
produce a close pi estimate; a third leaves the stored inverse-pi value
unchanged. A theoretical logarithmic error bound continues improving far beyond
that numerical plateau. An algebraically rationalised update exposes why
preserving a tiny auxiliary value alone does not certify the final pi estimate.
)

\toc

> A small execution: two improvements and one unchanged update

The seeds are \(y_0=\sqrt{2}-1\) and \(a_0=6-4\sqrt{2}\), with estimate \(p_0=1/a_0\).
The diagram follows an actual direct-evaluation trace requested for four
updates. The core stops after an update leaves its stored a unchanged.
The sequence API still returns five values, including the seed, by copying
the plateau value into later requested positions. A copied sequence position
is not an additional arithmetic update.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/borwein_states.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_trace(4);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.3;style.height=2;
  diagram.node("seed",{0,0},std::format("seed\ny = {:.2e}\npi = {:.12f}",trace.initial_y,trace.initial_estimate),style);
  std::string previous="seed";
  for (const auto& step:trace.steps) {
    const auto id=std::format("s{}",step.iteration);
    style.fill=step.unchanged?dm::RGB{255,237,213}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
    diagram.node(id,{4.4*step.iteration,0},std::format("update {}\ny = {:.2e}\npi = {:.12f}",
        step.iteration,step.y,step.estimate),style).edge(previous,id);
    previous=id;
  }
  diagram.text({6.6,2},"Actual direct-evaluation updates; node pi values are rounded for display")
      .text({6.6,-2},std::format("requested = {}; performed = {}; stored a unchanged = {}",
          trace.requested_iterations,trace.iterations_performed,trace.stagnated))
      .save_svg("build/borwein_states.svg");
  std::print("Requested updates: {}; actual updates: {}; stagnated: {}.\n\n",
      trace.requested_iterations,trace.iterations_performed,trace.stagnated);
  std::print("Seed estimate: {:.17g}; final estimate: {:.17g}.\n",trace.initial_estimate,trace.estimate);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Requested updates: 4; actual updates: 3; stagnated: true.

Seed estimate: 2.9142135623730985; final estimate: 3.1415926535897918.
\image(figures/borwein_states.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Green nodes are actual updates that change the stored inverse-pi value. The orange update is performed and recorded, then stops the loop. This is numerical stagnation, not proof that pi has been computed exactly.)
\alttext(A seed near 2.91421 leads to an estimate near 3.141592646, then near 3.141592654. A third orange update has auxiliary y zero and the same displayed estimate. The trace reports three performed updates out of four requested.)

> The recurrence and where fourth order appears

With \(r_k=(1-y_k^4)^{1/4}\), use

$$
y_{k+1}=\frac{1-r_k}{1+r_k},\qquad
 a_{k+1}=a_k(1+y_{k+1})^4
 -2^{2k+3}y_{k+1}(1+y_{k+1}+y_{k+1}^2),\qquad
 p_k=\frac1{a_k}.
$$

The native loop evaluates the fourth root with pow and forms the integer powers
by multiplication. Its seed, direct expression and short-run arithmetic order
are preserved from the original implementation.

For small \(u\), Taylor expansion gives \((1-u)^{1/4}=1-u/4+O(u^2)\). Substituting
\(u=y_k^4\) into the first recurrence gives

$$
y_{k+1}=\frac{y_k^4}{8}+O(y_k^8).
$$

Thus the auxiliary y shrinks with fourth order. Establishing the limit p_k=pi
also uses the special seed and the coupled a update; the local Taylor expansion
alone does not prove that limit. The iteration is given in Borwein and
Borwein's *Pi and the AGM*, Iteration 5.3. Brent's
[Algorithm BB4 and Corollary 1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.07558) connect its
exact-arithmetic estimates to every second Gauss–Legendre lower estimate.

> Original four-update sequence: discrepancy from rounded double pi

Use the original request for four updates. The reference is C++'s rounded
double pi constant, not an exact real pi oracle. An ULP here means the gap
from that constant to its next larger double. All displayed estimates lie
in the same [2,4) binade, so their signed gaps in these units are integer ULP
steps. Neither a small discrepancy nor equality to that reference certifies
all decimal digits or exact equality to pi.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/borwein_pi.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto values=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_sequence(4);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_trace(4);
  const double reference=std::numbers::pi;
  const double ulp=std::nextafter(reference,std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity())-reference;
  std::vector<double> iteration,accuracy;
  std::size_t zeros=0;
  std::print("| k | estimate | distance to double pi | signed local ULP gap | source of this value |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (std::size_t k=0;k<values.size();++k) {
    const double discrepancy=std::abs(values[k]-reference);
    const std::string event=k==0?"seed":k>trace.steps.size()?"padded copy":trace.steps[k-1].unchanged?"unchanged update":"update";
    std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.3e} | {:.0f} | {} |\n",k,values[k],discrepancy,(values[k]-reference)/ulp,event);
    if (discrepancy>0) {iteration.push_back(k);accuracy.push_back(-std::log10(discrepancy));}
    else ++zeros;
  }
  const double ulp_accuracy=-std::log10(ulp);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(iteration,accuracy,"direct discrepancy",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(iteration,accuracy,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .lines({0,4},{ulp_accuracy,ulp_accuracy},"one local ULP",dm::RGB{148,163,184},1)
      .title("Discrepancy from rounded double pi reaches a plateau")
      .x_label("requested update index k").y_label("-log10(abs(estimate - double pi))")
      .y_limits(0,20).size(900,500).save_svg("build/borwein_pi.svg");
  std::print("\nZero discrepancies omitted from the transform: {}. Local ULP: {:.3e}.\n",zeros,ulp);
}
```
