\image(figures/borwein_pi.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original direct values are retained, including a plateau three ULPs below the rounded double reference on this build. The ordinate is an explicitly transformed discrepancy, with no artificial positive error floor and no claim of an empirical quartic-order fit.)
\alttext(The negative decimal logarithm of the discrepancy rises from below one to about eight, then about fifteen, and remains flat. A horizontal reference marks the accuracy corresponding to one local ULP.)

The seed subtraction also loses relative precision: a_0 subtracts two nearby
rounded quantities. Later rounding is not repaired merely by requesting more
iterations. Once y is tiny, subtracting y^4 from one can round to one; its
fourth root is then one, and the direct numerator 1−r becomes zero.
The next block separates an exact-arithmetic model from these rounded states.

> Exact-arithmetic error bound, evaluated in the log domain

For exact iterates, Brent's equation (29) gives

$$
0<\pi-p_k<B_k,\qquad
 B_k=\pi^2 4^{k+2}\exp(-2\pi4^k).
$$

The native helper approximately evaluates its logarithm:

$$
\log_{10}B_k=2\log_{10}\pi+(k+2)\log_{10}4
 -\frac{2\pi4^k}{\ln10}.
$$

It avoids exponentiating an extremely small bound. Taking the negative
logarithm exposes a leading term proportional to 4^k; an additional exact
iteration therefore approximately quadruples the available decimal accuracy.
This bound is for exact arithmetic. It does not bound errors introduced by
rounded seeds, roots and updates, and its double evaluation is not interval
arithmetic or a certified numerical enclosure.
[Brent, equation (29) and Corollary 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.07558).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/borwein_exact_model.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto values=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_sequence(4);
  std::vector<double> index,model,observed_index,observed;
  std::print("| exact-arithmetic index k | approximate log10(B_k) | negative log10(B_k) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int k=0;k<=4;++k) {
    const double logarithm=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_log10_error_bound(k);
    if (!std::isfinite(logarithm)) return 1;
    index.push_back(k);model.push_back(-logarithm);
    const auto discrepancy=std::abs(values[k]-std::numbers::pi);
    if (discrepancy>0) {observed_index.push_back(k);observed.push_back(-std::log10(discrepancy));}
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",k,logarithm,-logarithm);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(index,model,"exact bound model",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2)
      .points(index,model,"",dm::RGB{22,163,74},4)
      .lines(observed_index,observed,"double discrepancy",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .title("Exact-arithmetic bound versus the double plateau")
      .x_label("update index k").y_label("negative log10(bound or reference discrepancy)")
      .y_limits(0,850).size(900,500).save_svg("build/borwein_exact_model.svg");
  std::print("\nThe index-four model remains representable as a logarithm even though B_4 is below double's positive range.\n");
}
```
