// result_end
\caption(The green model describes exact iterates; the blue curve describes discrepancies from a rounded double constant. Their separation after two updates is expected. Evaluating the model in doubles does not create an arbitrary-precision Borwein implementation.)
\alttext(The exact-arithmetic model rises from roughly half a decimal accuracy unit to eight, forty, one hundred seventy and nearly seven hundred. The double-reference discrepancy remains near fifteen.)

The proof and special seed come from the analytic theory of the arithmetic–
geometric mean; this report does not infer them from two double improvements.
The native tests independently compare early estimates with a long-double
Gauss–Legendre lower calculation. A separate 1000-digit development check uses
Machin's inverse-arctangent series as its pi reference, reproduces the doubled
AGM comparison and checks the theoretical bound. That oracle is validation,
not a dependency of the executable report.

> Rationalising next y and stopping before useless overflow

In exact arithmetic, with r^4=1−y^4,

$$
1-r=\frac{y^4}{1+r+r^2+r^3},\qquad
 y_{next}=\frac{y^4}{(1+r)(1+r+r^2+r^3)}.
$$

The rationalised mode evaluates that latter expression using the already
computed y^4. It can retain a positive next y even when rounded r is one.
Both modes intentionally share the original seed, so this experiment isolates
the next-y expression. Their final pi estimates can differ by a few ULPs;
preserving y alone does not certify the final reciprocal or reverse accumulated
rounding in a. The table contains only actual completed updates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
int main() {
  const double reference=std::numbers::pi;
  const double ulp=std::nextafter(reference,std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity())-reference;
  std::print("| expression | update | fourth root r | next y | root rounded to one | stored a unchanged | signed local ULP gap |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (auto mode:{dm::algorithms::BorweinEvaluation::direct,dm::algorithms::BorweinEvaluation::rationalised}) {
    const std::string name=mode==dm::algorithms::BorweinEvaluation::direct?"direct":"rationalised";
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_trace(4,mode);
    for (const auto& row:trace.steps)
      std::print("| {} | {} | {:.17g} | {:.6e} | {} | {} | {:.0f} |\n",name,row.iteration,row.fourth_root,
          row.y,row.root_rounded_to_one,row.unchanged,(row.estimate-reference)/ulp);
  }
  std::print("\n| requested updates | actual direct updates | finite result | estimate |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{0,4,512,std::numeric_limits<int>::max()}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_trace(n);
    if (!std::isfinite(trace.estimate)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.17g} |\n",n,trace.iterations_performed,std::isfinite(trace.estimate),trace.estimate);
  }
  const auto sequence=dm::algorithms::borwein_pi_sequence(1024);
  if (sequence.size()!=1025 || !std::isfinite(sequence.back())) return 1;
  std::print("\nA 1024-update sequence keeps its requested {} values by padding the finite plateau.\n",sequence.size());
}
```
