A 1024-update sequence keeps its requested 1025 values by padding the finite plateau.
// result_end
\caption(The direct expression produces y=0 at the rounded-root event; the rationalised expression retains a tiny positive y. Both stop when stored a ceases to change. Large scalar/trace requests retain a finite estimate.)

The old loop continued forming 2^(2k+3) after the estimate stopped changing.
At large k that scale overflows; multiplying infinity by the now-zero y
produced NaN. The shared loop now stops at the first unchanged stored a and
checks scale arithmetic. Trace records contain only actual updates, while
sequences preserve their original length by copying the final estimate.
Negative iteration requests retain the original lower clamp to zero.

For K performed updates the scalar uses O(K) fixed-precision operations and
O(1) auxiliary storage; trace storage is O(K). A requested sequence of N+1
values still takes O(N+1) time and output storage. This counts operations at
fixed double precision. Multiprecision multiplication, division and root
extraction have costs that depend on precision, so an iteration is not a
constant-cost operation when asking for arbitrarily many digits.

> References and reproducibility

J. M. Borwein and P. B. Borwein, *Pi and the AGM: A Study in Analytic Number
Theory and Computational Complexity*, Wiley, 1987, Iteration 5.3. The
[original-source discussion and references in Brent's paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.07558)
identify the seed and quartic iteration. R. P. Brent, *The Borwein Brothers,
Pi and the AGM*, arXiv:1802.07558, 2018, revised 2021, Algorithm BB4,
equation (29) and Corollaries 1–2, supplies the exact-arithmetic bound used here.

The input is the fixed mathematical seed and the original request for four
updates; there is no observational dataset or random seed. Every block is a
complete independent C++ programme using the canonical core. All seven public
documentation examples are compiled and run, actual state/sequence invariants
and extreme iteration requests have native tests, and the report's SVGs are
rendered into its local build directory before being copied into figures.
